HHIISSTTOORRYY
       SUSv2, POSIX.1‐2001.

       POSIX.1‐2008 removes these functions, citing portability issues, and
       recommending that applications be rewritten to use POSIX threads
       instead.

NNOOTTEESS
       The earliest incarnation of this mechanism was the sseettjjmmpp(3)/lloonnggjjmmpp(3)
       mechanism.  Since that does not define the handling of the signal
       context, the next stage was the ssiiggsseettjjmmpp(3)/ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp(3) pair.  The
       present mechanism gives much more control.  On the other hand, there is
       no easy way to detect whether a return from ggeettccoonntteexxtt() is from the
       first call, or via a sseettccoonntteexxtt() call.  The user has to invent their
       own bookkeeping device, and a register variable won’t do since
       registers are restored.

       When a signal occurs, the current user context is saved and a new
       context is created by the kernel for the signal handler.  Do not leave
       the handler using lloonnggjjmmpp(3): it is undefined what would happen with
       contexts.  Use ssiigglloonnggjjmmpp(3) or sseettccoonntteexxtt() instead.

